  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 335 AM EDT Wed Jul 1 2026\n\nMid-level high pressure will be centered over the Southern/Central \nAppalachians Thursday gradually shifting towards the Mid-Atlantic \nregion for the first half of the holiday weekend. On Sunday, the \nridge shifts east over the Atlantic bringing increased moisture to \nreturn to the area. \n\nHighs in the mid and upper 90s continue through Independence Day \nwith heat indices hovering in the 100-104 degree range. Chances for \nrain will generally be low given the isolated nature of any storms \nand the overall pattern, but do remain in the forecast. High \ninstability but a lack of deep layer shear will generally lead to \npulse type convection with storms capable of producing gusty wind, \npossibly up to severe levels with the strongest storms. \nAdditionally, heavy downpours may accompany any storm. Chances for \nrain increase Sunday into early next week with above normal moisture \nover the area. Rain chances and increased cloud cover bring forecast \ntemperatures down slightly Sunday and Monday but the majority of the \narea will still see highs from the low to mid 90s.",
